#. type: Title ==
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:48
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "Create the HAL pins."
msgstr "Créer les Widgets"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:57
msgid ""
"HAL pins are created with the normal Python \"hal\" library, and are not "
"specific to Vismach. Further details can be found in the "
"<<cha:halmodule,Creating Non-realtime Components in Python>> section.  A "
"component should be created with a name that matches the script file name "
"and then the HAL pins are added to that component. They will be referenced "
"by their component handle and short name when used to animate the Vismach "
"model."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:69
msgid ""
"Will create HAL pins 'samplegui.joint0' and 'samplegui.joint1'. When loading "
"the Vismach model with 'loadusr -W samplegui' the 'c.ready()' function tells "
"loadusr it's ready."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:76
msgid ""
"It is probably easiest to create geometry in a CAD package and *import* into "
"the model script with the `AsciiSTL()` or `AsciiOBJ()` functions.  Both "
"functions can take one of two named arguments, either a filename or raw data:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:84
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`part = AsciiSTL(filename=\"path/to/file.stl\")\n"
"part = AsciiSTL(data=\"solid part1 facet normal ....\")\n"
"part = AsciiOBJ(filename=\"path/to/file.obj\")\n"
"part = AsciiOBJ(data=\"v 0.123 0.234 0.345 1.0 ...\")`\n"
"The parts will be created in the Vismach space in the same locations as they occupy in the STL or OBJ space. This means that it may be possible to assemble the model in the CAD package."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:88
msgid ""
"Alternatively parts can be created inside the model script from a range of "
"*shape primitives*. Many shapes are created at the origin and need to be "
"moved to the required location after creation:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:94
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`cylinder = CylinderX(x1, r1, x2, r2)\n"
"cylinder = CylinderY(y1, r1, y2, r2)\n"
"cylinder = CylinderZ(z1, r1, z2, r2)`\n"
"Creates a (optionally tapered) cylinder on the given axis with the given radii at the given points on the axis."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:96
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`sphere = Sphere(x, y, z, r)`\n"
"Creates a sphere of radius r at (x,y,z)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:102
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`triangle = TriangleXY(x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3, z1, z2)\n"
"triangle = TriangleXZ(x1, z1, x2, z2, x3, z3, y1, y2)\n"
"triangle = TriangleYZ(y1, z1, y2, z2, y3, z3, x1, x2)`\n"
"Creates a triangular plate between planes defined by the last two values parallel to the specified plane, with vertices given by the three coordinate pairs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:104
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`arc = ArcX(x1, x2, r1, r2, a1, a2)`\n"
"Create an arc shape."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:107
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`box = Box(x1, y1, z1, x2, y2, z2)`\n"
"Creates a rectangular prism with opposite corners at the specified positions and edges parallel to the XYZ axes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:109
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`box = BoxCentered(xw, yw, zw)`\n"
"Creates an xw by yw by zw box centred on the origin."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:111
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`box = BoxCenteredXY(xw, yw, z)`\n"
"Creates a box of width xw / yw and height z."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:114
msgid ""
"Composite parts may be created by *assembling* these primitives either at "
"creation time or subsequently using `Collection()`:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:123
#, no-wrap
msgid "Moving Parts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:128
msgid ""
"Parts may need to be moved in the Vismach space to assemble the model.  They "
"may also need to be moved to create the animation as the animation rotation "
"axis is created at the origin (but moves with the Part):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:131
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`part1 = Translate([part1], x, y, z)`\n"
"Move part1 the specified distances in x, y and z."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:134
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`part1 = Rotate([part1], theta, x, y, z)`\n"
"Rotate the part by angle theta about an axis between the origin and x, y, z."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:144
msgid ""
"To animate the model (controlled by the values of HAL pins) there are two "
"functions 'HalTranslate' and 'HalRotate'. For parts to move inside an "
"assembly they need to have their HAL motions defined before being assembled "
"with the \"Collection\" command. The rotation axis and translation vector "
"move with the part as it is moved by the vismach script during model "
"assembly, or as it moves in response to the HAL pins as the model is "
"animated:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:147
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`part = HalTranslate([part], comp, \"hal_pin\", xs, ys, zs)`\n"
"The function arguments are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:150
msgid ""
"first a _collection/part_ which can be pre-created earlier in the script, or "
"could be created at this point if preferred eg `part1 = "
"HalTranslate([Box(....)], ...)`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:155
msgid ""
"The _HAL component_ is the next argument, i.e. the object returned by the "
"`comp = hal.component(...)` command. After that is the name of the HAL in "
"that will animate the motion, this needs to match an existing HAL pin that "
"is part of the HAL component created earlier in the script."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:161
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Then follow the _X, Y, Z scales_.\n"
"For a Cartesian machine created at 1:1 scale this would typically be 1,0,0 for a motion in the positive X direction.\n"
"However if the STL file happened to be in cm and the machine was in inches, this could be fixed at this point by using 0.3937 (1cm /2.54in) as the scale."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:165
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`part = HalRotate([part], comp, \"hal_pin\", angle_scale, x, y, z)`\n"
"This command is similar in its operation to HalTranslate except that it is typically necessary to move the part to the origin first to define the axis."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:170
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The _axis of rotation_ is from the origin point to the point defined by (x,y,z).\n"
"When the part is moved back away from the origin to its correct location the axis of rotation can be considered to remain \"embedded\" in the part."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:173
#, no-wrap
msgid "Assembling the model."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:180
msgid ""
"In order for parts to move together they need to be assembled with the "
"Collection() command. It is important to assemble the parts and define their "
"motions in the correct sequence. For example to create a moving head milling "
"machine with a rotating spindle and an animated draw bar you would:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:223
msgid ""
"Finally a single collection of all the machine parts, floor and work (if "
"any) needs to be created:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:230
msgid ""
"Thus, for example, in scaragui.py link3 is added to link2, link2 to link1 "
"and link1 to link0, so the final model is created by:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:237
msgid "Whereas a VMC model with separate parts moving on the base might have:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:250
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`part = Color([colorspec], [part])`\n"
"Sets the display color of the part. Note that unlike the other functions the part definition comes second in this case.\n"
"The colorspec consists of the three RGB values and an opacity. For example [1,0,0,0.5] for a 50% opacity red."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:253
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`myhud = Hud()`\n"
"Creates a heads-up display in the Vismach GUI to display such items as axis positions."
msgstr ""

#. Need to play around with this to see how it works.
#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:261
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`tooltip = Capture()`\n"
"Think of this as an invisible part that needs to be attached to the tooltip to track the position and orientation of the tool coordinate system. It is actually a transformation matrix that is constantly updated as the model moves."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:264
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`work = Capture()`\n"
"Same as above but attached to the work table to track the work coordinate system."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/gui/vismach.adoc:267
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`main(model, tooltip, work, size=10, hud=0, rotation_vectors=None, lat=0, lon=0)`\n"
"This is the command that makes it all happen, creates the display etc."
