#: man/man9/carousel.9:61
msgid "B<dir>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:63
msgid ""
"Set to 1 for unidirectional or 2 for bidirectional operation\\&. Default = "
"bidirectional"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:66
msgid "B<parity>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:68
msgid ""
"Set to 1 for odd parity, 0 for even parity checking\\&. Default = 0 (even)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:72
msgid ""
"This component is intended to help operate various types of carousel-type "
"toolchangers\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:74
msgid ""
"The component can be configured to operate with binary, binary-coded decimal "
"(BCD) or gray-coded position feedback (I<binary;, \\*(Aqbcd> and I<gray> "
"modes) It can alternatively work with an individual sensor for each tool "
"position (I<single> mode) or with a sensor at each tool position and a "
"separate index (I<index> mode)\\&. Systems using a stepper motor or "
"quadrature encoder are also supported (I<counts> mode)\\&. B<edge> is a "
"special case of index mode for tool changers with pockets on both the rising "
"and falling edges of the position sensor\\&. (Seen on at least one Denford "
"Orac\\&.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:76
msgid ""
"Both unidirectional and bidirectional systems are supported and those that "
"reverse against a stop when in position\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:78
msgid ""
"The number of carousel component instances created depends on the number of "
"entries in the I<pockets> modparam\\&. For example"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:80
msgid ""
"B<B loadrt carousel pockets=10,10,8>. Would create 3 carousel instances with "
"10, 10 and 8 pockets\\&. The other parameters are optional\\&. If absent "
"then defaults will be used\\&. Any missing entry will assume the previous "
"value\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:82
msgid ""
"When the enable pin is set to true the component will immediately set the "
"\"active\" pin to true and then (for a bidirectional instance) calculate the "
"shortest path to the requested pocket number\\&. The appropriate motor "
"direction output pins will then be set\\&. Bit outputs for forward and "
"reverse are provided as well as a three-state velocity output for driving a "
"DC motor PWM or a velocity-mode stepgen\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:84
msgid ""
"The component will monitor the carousel position and, when the correct "
"position is reached, set the motor-control pins to 0, set \"active\" to 0 "
"and set \"ready\" to 1\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:86
msgid ""
"In I<index>, I<edge> or I<counts\\(cqmode there is a need to find the "
"initial home position of the carousel\\&. The first time that the \"enable\" "
"pin is set; the carousel will rotate forwards searching for a home "
"signal\\&. In \\*(Aqindex> and I<edge> mode this is when both the index and "
"pulse inputs are true\\&. In I<counts> mode only the index input needs to be "
"set to set home\\&. Additionally in I<counts> mode the usual index-enable "
"logic of the encoder counters is supported\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:88
msgid ""
"With some carousel designs the carousel will not stop immediately\\&. To "
"allow for this set the B<align-dc> pin to a low velocity to be used for a "
"final latching move, and set the B<decel-time> to a suitable value\\&. Once "
"the decel-time has expired the carousel will, if it was moving forwards, "
"reverse back on to the position marker, off of the marker and then back on "
"to the FWD edge\\&. If moving in reverse it will continue off the marker and "
"then reverse slowly on to the FWD edge\\&. This alignment is only possible "
"with a motor-vel controlled bidirectional carousel, Other combinations will "
"be accepted but probably will not have the desired behaviour\\&. Some tuning "
"will be needed of align-dc and decel-time to achieve reliable operation\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:90
msgid ""
"In the unusual case that the index and pulse signals do not align it is "
"possible to use HAL logic to achieve the desired pin switching during "
"homing\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:92
msgid ""
"Setting \"enable\" low does not halt the homing move, so if homing on first "
"tool change is not needed then the enable pin can be toggled by an axis "
"homing pin or a script and the homing process will continue even if that "
"driving signal resets during the carousel homing move\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:94
msgid ""
"To operate the component with an encoder or stepgen use mode \"C\"\\&. The "
"B<scale> pin should be the number of steps or encoder counts between pocket "
"centres\\&. The B<width> pin can be used to stop the motor some distance "
"before the centre of the pocket to allow the motor time to decelerate\\&. In "
"mode \"C\" it is possible to use either the speed/direction control used in "
"other modes or to use direct position mode using the counts-target pin\\&. "
"The internal scaling of the encoder or stepgen should be set to 1\\&.0\\&. A "
"PID hal component will be needed for encoder applications whereas stepgen "
"configurations can use a stepgen in position control mode or in velocity "
"control mode with a PID\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: man/man9/carousel.9:96
msgid ""
"For tool changers which lock the carousel against a stop the B<rev-pulse> "
"pin can be set to a non-zero value\\&. The motor-rev pin will then be set "
"for this many seconds at the completion of the tool search and at the same "
"time the reverse duty/cycle velocity value will be sent to the motor-vel "
"pin\\&."
msgstr ""

